{"name":"Ritual Experience","hex":"#533844","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ritual","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Related to a rite or repeated set of actions."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rite; a repeated set of actions, especially in religious contexts (that is, a service)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ritual"},{"term":"experience","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The effect upon the judgment or feelings produced by any event, whether witnessed or participated in; personal and direct impressions as contrasted with description or fancies; personal acquaintance; actual enjoyment or suffering.","An activity one has performed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To observe certain events; undergo a certain feeling or process; or perform certain actions that may alter one or contribute to one's knowledge, opinions, or skills."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/experience"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ritual","blocks":[{"text":"A ritual is a repeated, structured sequence of actions or behaviors that alters the internal or external state of an individual, group, or environment, regardless of conscious understanding, emotional context, or symbolic meaning. Traditionally associated with gestures, words, or revered objects, rituals also occur in non-human species, such as elephant mourning or corvid object-leaving. They may be prescribed by tradition, including religious practices, and are often characterized by formalism, traditionalism, rule-governance, and performance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Gandrung_banyuwangi.jpg/330px-Gandrung_banyuwangi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ritual","wikidata":"Q189819"},{"title":"Experience","blocks":[{"text":"Experience refers to conscious events in general, more specifically to perceptions, or to the practical knowledge and familiarity that is produced by these processes. Understood as a conscious event in the widest sense, experience involves a subject to which various items are presented. In this sense, seeing a yellow bird on a branch presents the subject with the objects \"bird\" and \"branch\", the relation between them and the property \"yellow\". Unreal items may be included as well, which happens when experiencing hallucinations or dreams. When understood in a more restricted sense, only sensory consciousness counts as experience. In this sense, experience is usually identified with perception and contrasted with other types of conscious events, like thinking or imagining. In a slightly different sense, experience refers not to the conscious events themselves but to the practical knowledge and familiarity they produce. Hence, it is important that direct perceptual contact with the external world is the source of knowledge. So an experienced hiker is someone who has actually lived through many hikes, not someone who merely read many books about hiking. This is associated both with recurrent past acquaintance and the abilities learned through them."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experience","wikidata":"Q164359"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/IteMissaEst.jpg/500px-IteMissaEst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/IteMissaEst.jpg/500px-IteMissaEst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The use of Latin in a Tridentine Catholic Mass is an example of a \"restricted code\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIteMissaEst.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/The_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg/500px-The_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/The_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg/1280px-The_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/The_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg/500px-The_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The First Thanksgiving 1621, oil on canvas by Jean Leon Gerome Ferris (1863–1930). The painting shows common misconceptions about the event that persist to modern times: Pilgrims did not wear such outfits, and the Wampanoag are dressed in the style of Plains Indians.[16]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_First_Thanksgiving_cph.3g04961.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Ritual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_%28Detail%29.jpg/500px-Ritual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_%28Detail%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Ritual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_%28Detail%29.jpg/1280px-Ritual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_%28Detail%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Ritual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_%28Detail%29.jpg/500px-Ritual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_%28Detail%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ritual practitioner on Inwangsan Mountain, Seoul, South Korea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARitual_Practitioner_on_Inwangsan_Mountain_(Detail).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Codex_Magliabechiano_%28141_cropped%29.jpg/500px-Codex_Magliabechiano_%28141_cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or